<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiber River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tiber</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tiber is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> that flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a>. Rome grew up at a crossing of the river, with access to the coast and to inland trade routes. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> note the city's origins on the Tiber as part of the foundation of Roman power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiber-river.jpg" alt="Tiber River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rabax63 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PonteSantAngeloRom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Tiber is the third-longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the longest in central Italy. It rises at Mount Fumaiolo in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennine Mountains</a> in Emilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and flows 406 km through Tuscany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, where the River Aniene joins it, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a> between Ostia and Fiumicino. Its mouth has advanced by about 3 km since Roman times, which left the ancient port of Ostia Antica 6 km inland. The name is probably pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and may be Italic in origin; the same root appears in the Latin name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to legend, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was founded in 753 BCE on the banks of the Tiber, about 25 km from the sea. Tiber Island was the site of an important ford and was later bridged. Legend says the twins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> and Remus were abandoned on the river's waters and rescued by the she-wolf Lupa. The river marked the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> to the west, the Sabines to the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> to the south.</p><p>The Tiber was critically important to Roman trade, since ships could reach as far as 100 km upriver. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> of the third century BCE the harbour at Ostia became a key naval base, and it later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s most important port, receiving wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and wine from around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> linked the river to a sewer system, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloaca-maxima">Cloaca Maxima</a>. Heavy sedimentation made Ostia difficult to maintain, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> established a new port at Fiumicino in the first century CE; both ports were eventually abandoned because of silting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Tiber was long known for floods; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campus-martius">Campus Martius</a>, a flood plain, regularly flooded to a depth of about 2 m, and on 15 September 1557 the river rose 19 m above sea level and more than 1,000 people died. High stone embankments, begun in 1876, now confine it within the city. Ancient bridges survive in part, such as the Ponte Milvio and the Ponte Sant'Angelo, and in whole, as the Pons Fabricius. Because the river is identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the phrase crossing the Tiber has become shorthand for converting to Roman Catholicism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tiber” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Roman Empire replaced the Roman Republic, itself the product of centuries of expansion from a small city-state on the banks of the <b>Tiber</b> in central Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state was a city of perhaps 25,000 to 40,000 people on the <b>Tiber</b> in Latium, already one of the largest in central Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the language of the Latini, the people of Latium, the plain south of the <b>Tiber</b>, and of their most successful city, Rome.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome lay on Etruria’s southern border, across the <b>Tiber</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The portico's grey granite columns came from Mons Claudianus in Egypt and its pink ones from Aswan, each 11.9 metres tall and 60 tonnes, dragged to the Nile, shipped to Ostia and pulled up the <b>Tiber</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/pantheon">Pantheon · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Constantine's army defeated his rival Maxentius outside Rome, and Maxentius drowned in the <b>Tiber</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-28">28 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
